===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|defet}}, from {{der|en|fr|deffet}}, {{m|fro|desfait}}, past participle of the verb {{m|fro|desfaire}} (compare modern French {{m|fr|défaire}}), from {{m|fro|des-}} + {{m|fro|faire}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act or instance of being defeated, of being overcome or vanquished; a [[loss]].
#: {{ux|en|Licking their wounds after a temporary '''defeat''', they planned their next move.}}
# The act or instance of defeating, of [[overcome|overcoming]], [[vanquish]]ing.
#: {{ux|en|The inscription records her '''defeat''' of the country's enemies in a costly war.}}
# [[frustration|Frustration]] (by prevention of success), [[stymieing]]; {{lb|en|legal}} [[nullification]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1909|title=The Southern Reporter|page=250
|passage=... is subsequently issued to him, in accordance with his perfect equity thus acquired, by a legal fiction which the law creates for the protection, but not for the '''defeat''', of his title.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Gene Porter|title=A Daughter of the Land|publisher=volume 1|isbn=1442910054|page=17
|passage=She could see no justice in being forced into a position that promised to end in further humiliation and '''defeat''' of her hopes.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[destruction|Destruction]], [[ruin]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|4|1|text=and made '''defeat''' of her virginity}}

